                    briefing_text_r1:       `Two years of strong construction demand and a stabilizing peso have pushed effective utilization to <strong>~92% at peak</strong>. But the market-intelligence team is flashing red: cement dispatches are down two quarters, the central bank is tightening, mortgage approvals are softening, and the sovereign spread blew out <strong>250bps</strong> last month. A typical Argentine construction downturn cuts long-steel volume 20–30% within a year — the last one cut it 35%. Form a defensible view of <em>where in the cycle the market is</em>, and build a downside scenario with a stated probability and timing. The intel view: a downturn within <strong>12–18 months</strong>. Did Argentina finally stop having cycles, or are you reading the same signals your survivors remember from 2001–02?`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Time the capacity investment`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `The board is pressing to approve the <strong>US$140M (AR$168,000M)</strong> third rolling line + furnace upgrade — <strong>+40% capacity</strong>. But it takes <strong>24 months</strong> to commission, so it comes online <em>into the projected trough</em>, not the boom: build at the peak and you commission at ~55% utilization with a US$140M debt bill, and ROIC falls from 12% toward 4%. The supplier's price and slot hold for <strong>45 days</strong>, then +8% cost and +6 months delivery. Approve now, phase it (real option), defer to the trough, or decline — and crucially, run the NPV and ROIC against <em>trough</em>, not peak, EBITDA. When demand is procyclical and lead times are long, <strong>when</strong> you invest can matter more than how much.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Set the financial posture`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Funding the expansion fully with US$ debt pushes net leverage to <strong>~3.4x on peak EBITDA</strong> — against a <strong>3.5x covenant</strong>. Now run it on trough EBITDA, down 30%: leverage is <strong>~5.0x</strong>, you're in breach, and the bank can accelerate. The expansion doesn't kill you — the balance sheet does. Set the financing mix, a net-leverage ceiling judged on <em>trough</em> earnings, a minimum liquidity buffer sized to the downside, and FX hedging: you've signed a US$-priced order against AR$ revenues, and a 30% peso move would add AR$50,000M to the CapEx. <em>Debt capacity must be measured against trough, not peak, earnings — and the dry powder you preserve now is what funds the move in Round 4.</em>`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Make the competitive move`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Now choose one competitive move for the cycle and present it to the board. The larger incumbent has announced its own expansion; a smaller, over-leveraged rival is rumoured near distress and acquirable at the bottom for <strong>≈US$60M</strong>. Match the incumbent's expansion, hold and defend share on cost and service, or preserve <strong>dry powder</strong> to acquire the distressed rival at the bottom. But the option only exists if you <em>pre-committed</em> the balance-sheet capacity: if you're already fully levered with no liquidity buffer, the opportunity arrives and you have no headroom to take it. Reconcile the move with the capacity and leverage you already set — or watch the incumbent buy the rival and take 58% share while you stayed "safe".`,

