                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_peak:          `<strong>That's the cycle read your survivors would recognise.</strong> Near the peak, with the indicators rolling over — exactly when the temptation to build is strongest and the danger is greatest. Now make sure the capacity and leverage decisions actually act on that read, instead of contradicting it.`,
                    coach_r1_mid:           `<strong>"Mid-cycle with room to run" is what every peak feels like from the inside.</strong> Cement is down two quarters and the central bank is tightening — that is not the signature of a market with room to run. What would have to be true for you to be right, and how would you know if you were wrong?`,
                    coach_r1_early:         `<strong>"Strong demand is the new normal" is the trap.</strong> You projected today's volumes flat for five years — but the sovereign spread blew out 250bps last month. Did your plan account for that, or did you assume Argentina finally stopped having cycles?`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp cycle diagnosis.</strong> Peak read, all four indicators built into one system, and a real probability on the downturn. That is the disciplined cycle reading that lets every later decision be timed to the cycle that's coming — not the one you're in.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>Extrapolating current demand as trend is Common Error number two.</strong> Cement dispatches are down two quarters, the central bank is tightening, and the spread blew out 250bps. Which of those did your flat-for-five-years plan account for?`,
                    coach_r1_lowprob:       `<strong>A near-zero downturn probability ignores your own intel.</strong> The leading indicators point to a contraction within 12–18 months. If you price the downturn at almost nothing, every capacity and leverage decision downstream will be sized for a boom that's ending.`,
                    coach_r2_approve:       `<strong>Board approved — and the line commissions in 24 months, straight into a market that just contracted 30%.</strong> You now have 40% more capacity, a US$140M debt bill, and utilization at 55%. Your ROIC went from 12% to 4%. Did you build for the cycle you're in, or the one that's coming?`,
                    coach_r2_phase:         `<strong>Phasing it is the real-options play.</strong> Commit the furnace upgrade, hold the rolling line as an option, and decide the rest as uncertainty resolves. You keep the right to act later under less uncertainty — which is exactly what volatility this high makes valuable.`,
                    coach_r2_defer:         `<strong>Deferring to the trough is counter-cyclical discipline.</strong> Build at the bottom, cheaper, into the next upswing rather than into the contraction. The risk is being capacity-short in the recovery — so be ready to move fast when the cycle turns.`,
                    coach_r2_decline:       `<strong>Declining protects ROIC today</strong> — but if the next upswing comes and you're capacity-short while the incumbent expanded, you'll wish you'd at least held the option. Safe on returns; thin on growth optionality.`,
                    coach_r2_rush:          `<strong>You signed the full US$140M order this week to beat a 45-day deadline — at the peak.</strong> Speed bought you the slot and surrendered the flexibility. A refundable deposit would have held the option without committing the whole bill into the trough.`,
                    coach_r2_troughbasis:   `<strong>Running the NPV on trough EBITDA is the right discipline</strong> — but you still approved the full build at the peak. The math is honest; the timing isn't. Stressing the return against the downturn it commissions into should have changed the decision, not just the spreadsheet.`,
                    coach_r2_peakbasis:     `<strong>You ran the return on peak EBITDA.</strong> Today's strong numbers projected forward will justify almost any CapEx — that's exactly how peak investments get approved. Run it against the trough it commissions into and see if it still clears.`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>A balanced mix with an equity sleeve in reserve is the resilient posture.</strong> You keep the cost of capital reasonable without betting the covenant on US$ debt. Now hold the leverage ceiling on trough earnings and size the buffer to the downside.`,
                    coach_r3_usd:           `<strong>Funding the expansion on US$ debt is the cheapest headline — and the trap.</strong> You're at 3.4x on peak EBITDA against a 3.5x covenant. Run it on trough EBITDA, down 30%: 5.0x, in breach, bank can accelerate. What was your trough leverage, and did anyone calculate it?`,
                    coach_r3_local:         `<strong>Local AR$ debt currency-matches your revenues</strong> — but it's expensive and short-tenor, and it still adds leverage onto a balance sheet about to face a trough. It solves the FX problem at the cost of the rate and the maturity wall.`,
                    coach_r3_cash:          `<strong>Spending the balance sheet to cash to avoid debt feels prudent</strong> — but it consumes the very dry powder that would let you buy the rival at the bottom. Avoiding leverage and forfeiting the counter-cyclical option can be two faces of the same mistake.`,
                    coach_r3_loose:         `<strong>A ceiling above 3.5x on trough earnings is the covenant trap.</strong> A limit calibrated to good times becomes a breach the moment EBITDA falls. The covenant doesn't care what your leverage looks like at the peak — it tests you at the trough.`,
                    coach_r3_nobuffer:      `<strong>Zero liquidity buffer leaves you no dry powder for the bottom.</strong> When the distressed rival actually becomes available in the trough, you'll have no cushion and no headroom to act. You may be about to pre-spend the means to take the opportunity you'll want in Round 4.`,
                    coach_r3_unhedged:      `<strong>You signed a US$140M order with AR$ cash flows and no hedge.</strong> The peso moves 30% before the second progress payment and your CapEx just rose AR$50,000M in local terms. Was that a strategy decision, or a currency bet bolted onto one?`,
                    coach_r4_acquirewin:    `<strong>Buy at the bottom — and you kept the means to do it.</strong> The dry powder and the debt headroom you pre-committed in Round 3 are exactly what let you take the US$60M rival when it became available. The cycle just became a strategic weapon, not a hazard.`,
                    coach_r4_acquirenomeans:`<strong>Great instinct — buy at the bottom. But you're already fully levered with no buffer.</strong> When the rival actually became available, you had no dry powder and no debt headroom to act. You saw the opportunity and pre-spent the means to take it. How would you have kept the option alive?`,
                    coach_r4_match:         `<strong>Matching the incumbent capacity-for-capacity at the peak is escalation into the trough.</strong> Two new lines commissioning into a 30% contraction is how an industry destroys its own returns. You answered their move instead of timing yours.`,
                    coach_r4_defend:        `<strong>Holding and defending share on cost and service is solid through the trough.</strong> It protects ROIC and survival without overreaching — but it leaves the counter-cyclical prize on the table if you had the dry powder to take it.`,
                    coach_r4_retreat:       `<strong>Fortress balance sheet, zero moves.</strong> But the incumbent expanded, took your peak-demand orders, and the distressed rival you could have bought for US$60M was just acquired by your largest competitor — who now has 58% share. Safe from what, exactly?`,
                    coach_r4_integrated:    `<strong>That's the integrated play the whole simulation was building to.</strong> Cycle read, capacity timed off the peak, leverage held on trough earnings, dry powder pre-committed — and now the US$60M acquisition at the bottom that resilience was always meant to fund. The balance sheet was the weapon.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>"The balance sheet will catch up later" is how the move and the means come apart.</strong> A counter-cyclical play requires pre-committed capacity — you can't decide to have dry powder after the opportunity has already arrived.`,
                    coach_breach:           `<strong>You're in covenant breach.</strong> Net leverage on trough EBITDA is above 3.5x and the bank can accelerate. From here, every move is made from weakness — the expansion didn't kill you; the balance sheet did.`,

