                    insight_bottom:         `Bought at the bottom: you solved the cycle as one system — capacity timed off the peak, leverage held on trough earnings, FX hedged, and dry powder pre-committed precisely to acquire the distressed rival at the bottom. That is Vassolo's lesson — in volatile markets, the cycle is a strategic instrument, and the balance sheet is what lets you wield it.`,
                    insight_breach:         `Covenant breach: net leverage on trough EBITDA cleared 3.5x and the bank could accelerate. The expansion didn't kill you — the balance sheet did. Debt capacity has to be judged on trough, not peak, earnings; a covenant calibrated to good times is a trap when EBITDA falls.`,
                    insight_short:          `You cleared part of the cycle but not all of it. The capacity, leverage and competitive decisions still don't fully reinforce each other — enough slack for the trough, the covenant or the incumbent to find the seam.`,
                    insight_leverage:       `Your trough leverage is above the covenant. Returns and debt capacity must be assessed against trough EBITDA, not peak — the moment demand contracts 30%, peak-sized debt becomes a breach.`,
                    insight_resilient:      `Resilient balance sheet: you kept trough leverage low and preserved liquidity. That conservatism entering the peak is exactly what lets a firm buy assets and share cheaply at the bottom, instead of being bought.`,
                    insight_unhedged:       `You left US$-priced CapEx unhedged against AR$ revenues. In an emerging market, FX exposure is inseparable from the capacity and leverage decision — a 30% peso move turns a strategy decision into a currency bet.`,
                    insight_sidelined:      `Strong on survival, weak on position: a fortress balance sheet that took no position forfeits the counter-cyclical opportunity resilience was meant to create. Safe from what, exactly?`,
                    insight_roic:           `ROIC collapsed: capacity that commissions into the trough at low utilization is the classic emerging-market value-destroyer. Over-building at the peak is precisely the error procyclical demand punishes.`,
                    insight_weapon:         `You treated the cycle as a strategic weapon, not just a hazard: resilience entering the peak, discipline through the trough, and a counter-cyclical move at the bottom — capacity and share won cheaply when prices and multiples were lowest.`,
